WIP

How can I ensure that my code, program, or software is functional and secure?
  
To ensure that whatever project I am working on is functional, one key step is to perform testing on various aspects of the project.  For this course's work, I primarily implemented unit testing to ensure that each individual component worked as expected and hit the requirements set for each specific service.  To ensure that a project would be secure, I would follow best practices like input validation, using proper authentication methods, as well as encrypting sensitive data.  If I was using any third party dependencies or libraries, I would also make sure that those are updated when needed to ensure that no vulnerabilities are left in the system.

How do I interpret user needs and incorporate them into a program?

Interpreting user needs and properly incorporating them is extremely important.  Depending how how I gathered user requirements and needs, my approach for interpreting them to ensure I understand them might be a little bit different.  If I or someone else performed an interview, I would listen to that interview multiple times and take thorough notes and/or review someone else's notes.  If there is a requirements document or like a rubric as there was with this class, I would thouroughly review the requirements and even use comments at the start of a section of code to make a note of what the code is supposed to do or what attributes it needs to accomplish.  I incorporate those needs into a program by creating some form of a prototype with how I am "thinking" the end design will work and function, present that to a user, and then go off of that prototype because I would know how that would be implemented.  I would also use good software design practices in term of both function and security.

How do I approach designing software?
I approach designing software by first ensuring that I have a good understanding of what the user is wanting in the software.  If I do not know that information, I cannot properly develop software that the user is wanting and most likely will end up having to redesign large portions of the software.  When I am actually coding the product, I will typically get some form of a working design to ensure that I know I can make the software do what is asked and then work to refine it, make it more efficient, and make it more secure.
